Every change to skoro is an **entry** in the [archive](../../archive/), worked on in a branch of
its own and moved through a fixed series of **stages**. Each stage produces something (a document,
the code, or the pages), and ends only when a person approves it; nothing moves on by itself. This
page describes the whole workflow: the kinds of entry, the stages each goes through, the commands
that move an entry between them, and what each stage leaves behind.

The work of a stage is usually done by a coding agent, such as Claude Code, which a person drives
with the `/wf-…` commands below. Moving an entry (approving a stage, going back, setting the entry
aside or taking it up again) is always the person's to ask for.

## Three kinds of entry

An entry is opened with `/wf-start <slug> <kind>`, and its kind fixes the stages it goes through.

| kind         | for                                                         | stages                                              |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| **feature**  | an algorithm, a distribution, an example                    | mathematics, implementation, plan, build, publish   |
| **chore**    | a port or a refactor: no new mathematics, no new design      | plan, build, publish                                |
| **decision** | a position the library takes, argued in prose               | writeup, publish                                    |

The route is fixed by the kind. A feature always goes through all five of its stages, in order:
`/wf-start` opens it at its mathematics, and each approval moves it exactly one stage on, so no
stage can be skipped, and none can be approved empty. Work that needs no new mathematics and no
new design is a chore, which starts at its plan; that is the only way to begin past the
mathematics.

## The flow

<ModelGraph spec={workflow} />

Every stage is worked on with its own command, and any number of times: a stage is a
conversation, in which its document (or code, or pages) is revised and committed until it is
right. When it is, `/wf-approve` accepts it and opens the next stage. `/wf-status` says at any
time which entry the branch works on, the stage it is at, and the command that comes next.

## The stages

1. **Mathematics** (`/wf-math`). A writeup of the mathematics at play, revised until it is right.
   Ideas that stand on their own become [math notes](../../math/), which later writeups build on.
2. **Implementation** (`/wf-implementation`). A companion that turns the mathematics into a
   design: the types and traits, sketched in code, where the neighbouring interfaces constrain
   them, and the tests that will show the design works. It is meant to be complete enough that
   someone who knows Rust, the mathematics and those interfaces could build it without designing
   anything of their own. It cannot be approved without its section of tests.
3. **Plan** (`/wf-plan`). The implementation's prose distilled into a checklist of tasks, each
   small enough for one commit and each naming its tests, in an order that keeps the code
   compiling and its tests passing after every task.
4. **Build** (`/wf-build`). The code, built task by task, each task committed with its box ticked
   in the plan, which is the only change the plan may receive once approved. It cannot be approved
   with a box unticked or work uncommitted. At its end, the build reports the order in which to
   read each crate it changed (see [What you can see along the way](#what-you-can-see-along-the-way)).
5. **Publish** (`/wf-publish`). The pages of the guide, the concepts and the reference are brought
   up to date, quoting the code that now exists, and the entry lists the pages and the crates it
   changed. Approving it finishes the entry.

A decision's **writeup** (`/wf-writeup`) takes the place of the first four: the argument for the
position, revised until it is right, and then published.

Each stage's document is frozen once approved, and the parts of the repository open for editing
as the entry advances: the math notes from the mathematics (or the writeup), the library's code
from the build, and the documentation pages at publishing. The entry's own folder is open
throughout.

## Going back, and setting aside

A stage's work sometimes shows an earlier stage wrong: a test shows the mathematics mistaken, or a
task cannot be built as designed. The work then stops, and `/wf-reopen <stage> <reason>` moves the
entry back to the earlier stage. The reason is recorded in the entry's history, the reopened
document gains a section of revisions saying what changed and why, and every stage from there on
is approved again on the way forward, unchanged if nothing in it had to change. A design is
allowed to change its story; it is not allowed to forget it.

An entry can be set aside with `/wf-abandon <reason>`. Its documents stay, approved or not, and
`/wf-resume <slug>` takes it up again at the stage where it stopped.

Once its publishing is approved, an entry is done, and a done entry is never edited again: it can
no longer be reopened. When skoro later changes what an entry built, a new entry says so, opened
with the earlier entry named as the one it revises. The archive then marks the earlier entry as
revised, and the pages it changed show both.

## What you can see along the way

Each kind of work is visible as it happens: the documents of an entry are pages of the
[archive](../../archive/) from the moment they are written, and the archive shows each entry's
stage and history.

The code has a view of its own. For every crate in the workspace, the site draws
[the order in which to read its modules](../../reading-order/), computed from the sources each time
the site is built: an order in which as little as possible is used before it is read, with the few
uses of modules not yet read named beside the modules that make them. Those pages follow the code
at every stage, and at the end of a build the agent prints the order of each crate the branch
changed, so the person approving the build can read what was built. When the entry is published,
its page in the archive links to the reading order of each crate it changed.

## What the stages guarantee

The stages are kept by a small tool, `bin/wf`, which refuses an edit the current stage does not
allow and checks the rules on every pull request:

- A branch works on one entry at a time, and merges only once that entry is done or set aside.
- An approved document is frozen until its stage is reopened, which is recorded, with its reason,
  in the entry's history.
- A document is never written before its stage, the code changes only once a plan is approved, and
  the pages that say what skoro is change only at the end, when there is code to quote.
- A stage is approved only when its work is committed and complete: no open questions left in a
  document, a test section in an implementation, a checklist in a plan with every box ticked by the
  end of the build, and, at publishing, every page and crate the branch changed listed by the
  entry.

The same procedure, written for the agents that carry it out, is in the repository's
[`AGENTS.md`](https://github.com/mvarble/skoro/blob/main/AGENTS.md) and the workflow reference it
points to.
